<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guernica</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gernika</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Guernica (Basque: Gernika) is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biscay">Biscay</a> in the Basque Country of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. Its oak tree was the meeting place of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juntas-generales-of-biscay">Juntas Generales of Biscay</a>, where the lords of Biscay swore to uphold the province's fueros, and it remains a symbol of Basque liberties. On 26 April 1937, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a>, German and Italian aircraft bombed the town, an attack commemorated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pablo-picasso">Picasso</a>'s painting Guernica.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guernica.jpg" alt="Guernica" width="320" height="499" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Papamanila · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arbol_de_Gernika_d%C3%ADa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
